WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.620
Not every pipeline should remain a single dense line.

2
00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:08.600
When a pipeline becomes complex, it may be safer to break it into named steps.

3
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:19.980
Intermediate results can be inspected, validated, logged at a safe level, or reused without forcing reviewers to mentally execute a long chain of commands.

4
00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:24.580
Temporary files can make pipelines clearer, but they must be handled safely.

5
00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:34.920
A script should create them in an appropriate location, use permissions suitable for the data, avoid predictable or shared unsafe names, and clean them up deliberately.

6
00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:38.620
Temporary data is still data, and it may need protection.

7
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:40.820
Intermediate data can be sensitive.

8
00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:49.080
It may contain secrets, customer data, operational details, deployment decisions, file inventories, or vulnerability reports.

9
00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:58.860
If that data would be sensitive in a normal file, it is sensitive in a temporary file, process substitution stream, captured variable, or build artifact.

10
00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:07.380
Process substitution can improve readability in some cases because it lets tools consume generated streams without forcing everything into one pipeline.

11
00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:14.280
It is still part of the data-flow design and should be reviewed for lifecycle, sensitivity, and compatibility assumptions.

12
00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:17.360
Avoid accidental artifact or log exposure.

13
00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:25.460
Intermediate files should not be copied into build outputs, left behind in workspaces, or dumped into diagnostic logs by default.

14
00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:31.080
A secure pipeline protects intermediate data with the same seriousness as final output.
